<BaseLayout title="The Hedgehog Who Went to Key West — Huanyu Chen"
  description="SIGGRAPH 2024 in Denver, then a two-week road trip: New York, New England, the Great Lakes, Chicago, Orlando and Key West.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · July–August 2024</p>
  <h1>The Hedgehog Who Went to Key West</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">SIGGRAPH 2024 in Denver, then New York to Key West, a city a day.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>18 days · July 28 – August 14, 2024</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → Denver (SIGGRAPH 2024) → New York → New England → the Great Lakes → Chicago →
    Orlando → Key West → Los Angeles<br />
    2,541 miles driven · 13 states by car, plus Colorado and Georgia · 13 nights on the road, only two places twice ·
    ten university campuses, mostly unplanned · one toy hedgehog as mascot
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.webp'} alt="Map of the whole trip: Los Angeles to Denver to New York, a driving loop through New England, the Great Lakes and Chicago, flights to Orlando, a drive to Key West, and flights home via Atlanta." /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole loop. Solid lines are driving; dashed lines are flights.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    It started with a conference. SIGGRAPH 2024 was in Denver, and when it ended I didn't fly home. I flew to New
    York, where the other half of this story's "we" was already waiting. We picked up a rental car at JFK on the evening
    of August 1st and gave ourselves two weeks. We didn't set out to
    make it a race. But when the goal is the southernmost point of the continental United States and the starting line
    is New York, you end up sleeping in a different bed almost every night. Thirteen nights, and only twice did we get
    to unpack for a second day. The rest was a city a day: coffee in one state, dinner in another.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll. A phone remembers where and when every photo was taken,
    and lined up in order, a few hundred pictures turn out to be a surprisingly honest diary. They record the
    detours we forgot, the hour we actually left a place, and how much of every day was spent looking up at buildings.
    Where the photos are silent, I've said so rather than fill the gap with a guess. Where a place has a story worth
    knowing, I've added a little of it, because half the fun of a trip like this is finding out afterwards what you
    were looking at.
  </p>
  <p>
    A word on the shape of it. The trip falls naturally into a prologue and three acts. The prologue is a week at a
    conference in Denver. Act I is a long, looping drive from New York through New England, upstate New York, the Rust
    Belt and the Great Lakes to Chicago, with a detour into Wisconsin. Act II is a flight south and two days in the theme
    parks of Orlando. Act III is a drive to the very end of the road in Key West, followed by an improbable afternoon in
    Atlanta on the way home. Through all of it, a small toy hedgehog rode on the dashboard. He joined in Vermont, and he
    has his own section at the end.
  </p>

  <h2>Prologue: SIGGRAPH in Denver</h2>
  <p>
    The trip began, as many graphics people's summers do, at <strong>SIGGRAPH</strong>, the annual conference on
    computer graphics and interactive techniques. It is the place where the people who build rendering engines,
    animation tools and simulation software meet the people who make films and games with them, and for one week a
    year it takes over a convention center. In 2024 that convention center was in Denver.
  </p>
  <p>
    I left Los Angeles, alone, on the morning of July 28th. Taking off westbound, the plane banked out over Santa
    Monica Bay before turning east. For a minute the whole coastline was laid out below: the beach towns, the runways
    of LAX and the sun glinting off the water. The first souvenir of the trip was bought in Denver that same
    afternoon. It was a Colorado fridge magnet, all elk, mountains and wildflowers, and it turned out to be the first of
    many. That week I stayed at the Embassy Suites by the convention center (it's in the <a href="/misc/hotels">hotels post</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lax-takeoff.webp'} alt="Aerial view of the Los Angeles coastline and LAX runways from a departing plane." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Leaving LA: the coast and LAX from above, July 28th.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'colorado-magnet.webp'} alt="A Colorado souvenir magnet with mountains, elk and wildflowers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Magnet number one.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Four days at the convention center</h3>
  <p>
    The conference opened that evening with a ritual. You file into a darkened hall the size of an aircraft hangar,
    thousands of people facing a wall of screens, the lights go down, and the screens fill with the year's best
    computer animation and visual effects, set to music loud enough to feel in your seat. Then the lights come up,
    and you spend the next four days doing the opposite: sitting in small rooms listening to people explain, equation by
    equation, how one tiny piece of it was done. The week at the <strong>Colorado Convention Center</strong>
    was all of that: talks, the exhibition floor, too many conversations to count, and a forty-foot blue bear peering in
    through the glass at all of it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'siggraph-keynote.webp'} alt="A packed, dark conference hall with large screens at SIGGRAPH 2024." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>SIGGRAPH 2024, opening evening.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The bear is officially called <em>I See What You Mean</em>. Lawrence Argent made it in 2005, and it has become the
    building's unofficial mascot and arguably the city's. It stands on the sidewalk on 14th Street with its paws and
    nose pressed against the glass, like a curious tourist trying to see what all the people inside are doing. Argent
    said the idea came from a photo of a bear peering into a window. The joke works from both sides. From the outside
    he looks in; from the inside he looms behind the giant SIGGRAPH letters in the lobby, a vast blue silhouette
    against the downtown towers. That makes him the most photographed attendee of the week. Every morning a small
    crowd of badge-wearers stood at his feet with their phones up, and I was one of them.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-blue-bear.webp'} alt="The giant blue bear sculpture leaning against the glass wall of the Colorado Convention Center." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>I See What You Mean</em>, the Big Blue Bear.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'siggraph-letters.webp'} alt="Large SIGGRAPH letters inside the convention center, with the blue bear visible through the window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same bear, from the inside.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Denver takes a day to get used to. The city sits a mile up, and the air is thin and dry in a way you notice
    on the first flight of stairs and in the first hour without a water bottle. The sunlight is harder and whiter than
    at sea level, shadows are sharp-edged, and the sky has the deep, saturated blue of high places. Every afternoon
    that week, big cumulus clouds built up over the mountains and drifted east over the city, so the photos from the
    concourse windows all have the same look: glass towers, blue sky, and towering white clouds that seem close enough to
    touch.
  </p>
  <p>
    The upper concourse has its own view: west across the city to the faint line of the Front Range, with the tall
    white figures of Jonathan Borofsky's <em>Dancers</em> spinning on a lawn below. There are two of them, sixty
    feet tall, mid-step outside the Denver Performing Arts Complex, and from above they look like a pair of
    paper-doll cutouts somebody stood up in the grass. The concourse also has a rooftop terrace with a view straight
    into the downtown towers. It was the best place in the building to eat lunch, and every time I looked, it was empty.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-dancers.webp'} alt="View from the convention center over a road and lawn to two tall white dancing-figure sculptures, with the mountains faint on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Borofsky's <em>Dancers</em> from the convention center, with the Front Range on the horizon.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the show floor, studios and labs set up booths, and research groups printed banners announcing how many papers
    they had gotten into the conference, the academic equivalent of a sports team hanging championship pennants. A
    camera company's booth was hung with glossy film posters, and people drifted between the stands with their badges
    swinging. That is SIGGRAPH's particular charm: the line between "a paper" and "a product" is very short, and the
    same aisle holds both. Next year's conference was already
    advertising itself on the show floor: SIGGRAPH 2025, Vancouver, August 10 to 14.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'siggraph-2025-teaser.webp'} alt="A booth on the exhibition floor advertising SIGGRAPH 2025 in Vancouver, August 10 to 14." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Already selling next summer.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Conference food is its own genre. Lunches were whatever was fastest along the 16th Street Mall, the mile-long
    pedestrian street that runs through downtown: a bento box one day, a poke bowl the next, a bowl of ramen on the
    third, with bubble tea as a recurring character. One evening I went out to the southern suburbs for Chinese food,
    fortune cookies included; another ended at an Indian restaurant a few blocks from the convention center. None of
    this is remarkable, and all of it is in the camera roll, which tells you something about what a conference week
    actually consists of.
  </p>

  <h3>An evening walk through the Civic Center</h3>
  <p>
    The best evening was a walk. Just after eight, as the light went orange, I headed south out of downtown. The route
    passed the curved glass front of the Denver Justice Center and the U.S. Mint, where every coin with a small "D" on
    it is made. Then came the columns of the old Carnegie library of 1909, now the McNichols Building, and from there
    <strong>Civic Center Park</strong> opened up.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-denver.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-denver.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Prologue: the evening walk of July 29, from the convention center to the art museum." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Prologue: the evening walk of July 29, from the convention center to the art museum.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Civic Center Park is one of the purest examples anywhere of the City Beautiful movement, the early-twentieth-century
    idea that grand civic architecture and formal landscaping would make cities more orderly and their citizens more
    civic-minded. The layout is a long axis of lawns and flower beds. At one end is the gold-domed <strong>Colorado
    State Capitol</strong>; at the other, facing it, is the curved neoclassical arc of the <strong>City and County
    Building</strong>. Along the sides stand a Greek amphitheater and a curved colonnade, the Voorhies Memorial, which
    at dusk frames the downtown towers like a theater set. The Capitol's dome is covered in real gold leaf, a tribute to
    the gold rush that founded the city. One of its front steps is engraved as being exactly one mile above sea level,
    though surveys have moved the true mile mark a couple of times since.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'colorado-capitol.webp'} alt="The Colorado State Capitol with its gold dome across Civic Center Park at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Colorado State Capitol from Civic Center Park.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-city-county.webp'} alt="Denver's City and County Building under orange sunset clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The City and County Building at sunset.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The sunset that evening was absurd. Low clouds behind the City and County Building's clock tower turned orange and
    then pink, and the building went dark against them, a perfect silhouette with a single lit window. Ten minutes later
    it was over, and the park's old globe lamps came on along the paths.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-colonnade.webp'} alt="The curved Voorhies Memorial colonnade in Civic Center Park at dusk, with flowers and a pink-tinged sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Voorhies Memorial colonnade in Civic Center Park.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The cultural district</h3>
  <p>
    South of the park is Denver's cultural district, and it is a sculpture garden that happens to have buildings in it.
    The <strong>Central Library</strong> is two buildings in one: a 1950s modernist block and Michael Graves's 1995
    addition, a stack of colored postmodern towers and pavilions that looks like a child's building blocks blown up to
    civic scale. Out front, behind a construction fence that summer, stood <em>The Yearling</em> by Donald Lipski, a
    painted pony on a giant red chair. It is a sculpture about reading: the chair is the kind you'd curl up in with a
    book, and the pony is the kind you'd read about. Across the plaza are a field of tall red columns and a huge dark
    curl of steel.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-library.webp'} alt="The postmodern towers of the Denver Central Library at dusk, in brick, stone and green." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Denver Central Library's Michael Graves addition.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-yearling.webp'} alt="A sculpture of a pinto pony standing on an enormous red chair outside the Denver Central Library." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>The Yearling</em>, outside the Denver Central Library.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    I ended at the <strong>Denver Art Museum</strong>. Its Hamilton Building, by Daniel Libeskind, opened in 2006 and
    looks like a titanium shard trying to take off. There is hardly a right angle on it; the walls lean, the roof
    points, and one wedge projects out over the street as if the building had been caught mid-explosion. Libeskind
    said he was inspired by the peaks of the Rockies, which you can just about believe at dusk, when the titanium turns
    the same gray-blue as the mountains. Its outdoor sculptures include <em>The Big Sweep</em>, a giant dustpan and
    broom by Claes Oldenburg and Coosje van Bruggen, who made a career of enlarging everyday objects until they become
    monuments.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-art-museum.webp'} alt="The angular titanium-clad Hamilton Building of the Denver Art Museum at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Denver Art Museum's Hamilton Building.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-big-sweep.webp'} alt="A giant blue dustpan and broom sculpture outside the Denver Art Museum." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>The Big Sweep</em>, outside the museum.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Nearby, a bronze cow lies in the grass with her calf, lit from below by a garden spotlight, looking far
    calmer than anything else in the neighborhood.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-cow.webp'} alt="A bronze sculpture of a cow lying in the grass beside her standing calf, at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A bronze cow and calf in the museum's sculpture garden.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Even a hotel lobby next door turned out to be a small gallery: a horse built from what looks like driftwood, a
    bronze girl holding a bouquet, and a tabletop model of that same dustpan and broom. Denver, it seems, does not let a
    blank wall or an empty lawn go to waste.
  </p>

  <h3>Heading east</h3>
  <p>
    On August 1st the conference wound down, and I caught a flight east to New York, where the road trip, and the "we",
    began. Denver International was packed with people heading home. The flight crossed the Plains over a patchwork of green circles. That is center-pivot irrigation:
    a long sprinkler arm rotating around a well, painting a perfect disc of green on otherwise tan land, seen from
    cruising altitude. Thousands of them tile Kansas and Nebraska, and from the air they look like a punch card.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'plains-pivots.webp'} alt="View from a plane window over the jet engine to farmland dotted with green center-pivot irrigation circles." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Center-pivot circles over the Plains.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By evening a white Hyundai sedan was waiting for us in a parking lot at JFK. It would carry us a little over two
    thousand miles in nine days. We drove it to Flushing for Hunan food and bubble tea, and then out of the city that
    same night, to the Connecticut shore and a Hampton Inn in Milford.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have never started a road trip from a rental lot at JFK, picture it like this. A few minutes ago you were
    in a crowded terminal; now you are in a car you don't know, adjusting mirrors in a parking lot that smells of jet
    fuel and hot asphalt, with New York City between you and everywhere you want to go. The car still has that rental
    smell. The phone is propped up with the map open. Somewhere ahead are two weeks and a few thousand miles, and
    none of it feels real yet. It starts to feel real a few hours later, when the city falls behind and the road starts
    to lead somewhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Act I: New York to Chicago, the long way</h2>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-northeast.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-northeast.webp'} alt="Detailed map of the drive from New York through New England, upstate New York, Pittsburgh, Ohio and Michigan to Chicago and Wisconsin, with numbered overnight stops." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Act I in detail: 2,147 miles and nine nights. Nx marks the night number.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The plan for Act I, to the extent there was one, was to drive the long way round from New York to Chicago. Instead
    of heading straight west across Pennsylvania, we would go north into New England, cut across upstate New York to
    Niagara, and then work west along the Great Lakes. It is more than twice the distance of the direct route, which was
    the point.
  </p>

  <h3>New Haven and Providence</h3>
  <p>
    The first stop was <strong>New Haven</strong>, at midday, for a walk through Yale. Yale's look, the dark stone,
    leaded windows, towers and archways that make it look older than Oxford, is largely the work of one architect,
    James Gamble Rogers. In the 1920s and 30s he built much of the campus in an intensely detailed Collegiate Gothic,
    and he went to great lengths to make it look ancient. Workers reportedly splashed acid on the stone to age it,
    broke and reset some of the window glass, and set the masonry slightly irregular. We walked past the Sterling
    Memorial Library, which is essentially a cathedral dedicated to books, and the tower of the Humanities Quadrangle.
    A twisted white sculpture on Broadway was the only thing in sight that looked like it belonged to this century.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0802.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0802.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1: from the Connecticut shore to Boston, by way of Yale and Brown." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: from the Connecticut shore to Boston, by way of Yale and Brown.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yale-hq.webp'} alt="Yale's Humanities Quadrangle, a Gothic stone building with a tall brick tower above an arched entrance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Yale: the tower of the Humanities Quadrangle.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From New Haven, I-95 runs east along the Connecticut shore, the sky that day a flat silver over the trees. You
    reach Providence the way most people do, on the elevated highway, with the downtown towers rising ahead and the
    hill of the old city behind them. By late afternoon we were climbing College Hill for a walk on Brown's campus. Brown
    has the opposite personality from Yale: red brick instead of gray stone, Georgian instead of Gothic, and a main green
    that feels like a New England town common. We came in through Faunce Arch onto the Main Green. On one side stands
    <strong>University Hall</strong>, built in 1770 and the oldest building on campus; during the Revolutionary War it
    served as a barracks and hospital for American and French troops. Sayles Hall is nearby. On Waterman Street, the
    bronze Brown Bear stands on his hind legs on a granite plinth, looking more alarmed than fearsome. We had boba on
    campus and stayed in Providence for hotpot before the drive to Boston.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brown-bear.webp'} alt="A bronze bear standing upright on a granite plinth on the Brown University campus." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Brown Bear, College Hill.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brown-university-hall.webp'} alt="University Hall, a four-story red-brick Georgian building with a cupola, across Brown's Main Green." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>University Hall (1770), Brown's oldest building.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Boston and the Freedom Trail</h3>
  <p>
    Then came two nights outside <strong>Boston</strong>, at a Hilton Garden Inn in Tewksbury, our first real breather. That full day started in Cambridge,
    with a walk around Harvard, a gelato, and a drive through MIT, past the labs and startups of Kendall Square, with the Boston skyline lined up across the
    Charles. From there the day became the Freedom Trail.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0803.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0803.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2: Cambridge in the morning, then the Freedom Trail on foot." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: Cambridge in the morning, then the Freedom Trail on foot.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The Freedom Trail is a two-and-a-half-mile line of red brick set into the sidewalks of Boston, connecting sixteen
    sites from the city's colonial and revolutionary history. It was the idea of a local journalist in 1951. It is the
    simplest possible piece of tourism infrastructure, a line on the ground that you follow, and it works brilliantly,
    because Boston's history is so densely packed that you rarely walk five minutes without hitting the next stop.
  </p>
  <p>
    Walking it is a particular pleasure. You look down to find the red line, then up to find the building, then down
    again, and the city comes at you in layers: a graveyard from the 1600s beside a glass office tower, a colonial
    meeting house at the foot of a skyscraper, a brass plaque about a riot outside a coffee shop. That
    Saturday the sky was enormous and blue, piled with white cumulus, and the trail was crowded with people
    walking it from end to end.
  </p>
  <p>
    It begins on Boston Common, the oldest public park in the country, where the squirrels were extremely tame. One
    sprawled on a granite curb like a cat on a warm day and watched us photograph it; another sat up in the grass a few
    feet away to see whether we had food. Just above the Common is the <strong>Massachusetts State House</strong>,
    designed by Charles Bulfinch and finished in 1798. Its dome was originally wood, then sheathed in copper by Paul
    Revere's company, and finally gilded. During the Second World War it was painted gray so it wouldn't catch the
    moonlight for enemy bombers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-state-house.webp'} alt="The Massachusetts State House with its gold dome." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Boston: the State House on Beacon Hill, along the Freedom Trail.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-squirrel.webp'} alt="A gray squirrel resting on a granite curb on Boston Common, looking at the camera." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Boston Common local.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From there the trail runs downhill past King's Chapel, a squat granite church from the 1750s. Then comes the
    <strong>Golden Teapot</strong>, a giant gilded kettle hanging over a street corner near Government Center. It was
    made in 1873 as a sign for the Oriental Tea Company, is said to hold over two hundred gallons, and still steams
    occasionally. Across the plaza is City Hall, a concrete monolith from 1968 that is either a masterpiece of
    brutalism or the ugliest building in America, depending on whom you ask. Bostonians have been asking for over
    fifty years.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-teapot.webp'} alt="A large gilded teapot sign hanging from a building corner in downtown Boston." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Golden Teapot, steaming since 1873.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-city-hall.webp'} alt="Boston City Hall, a massive stepped concrete building, under a blue sky with white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Boston City Hall (1968).</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The <strong>Old State House</strong> is the best visual joke on the trail. It is a small brick building from 1713
    with a pretty tower and the lion and unicorn of the British crown still on its roof, and it now stands in a canyon
    of glass towers that make it look like a dollhouse. The Declaration of Independence was read to Bostonians from its
    balcony in July 1776, and the Boston Massacre happened in the street right in front of it. Next comes
    <strong>Faneuil Hall</strong>, a market building from 1742 known as the Cradle of Liberty for the protest meetings
    held in it, with a statue of Samuel Adams standing out front, arms folded, looking as if he is about to object to
    something.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-old-state-house.webp'} alt="The small brick Old State House with its tower, surrounded by tall glass office buildings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Old State House, dwarfed by the Financial District.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-faneuil.webp'} alt="The statue of Samuel Adams in front of the red-brick Faneuil Hall under a dramatic cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Samuel Adams outside Faneuil Hall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By five o'clock we were in the North End, Boston's old Italian neighborhood, where Hanover Street was as crowded as
    you'd expect on a summer Saturday. There, Paul Revere rides in bronze down a tree-lined mall, with the white steeple
    of the Old North Church rising behind him.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-paul-revere.webp'} alt="A bronze equestrian statue of Paul Revere between rows of trees, with the white steeple of the Old North Church behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Paul Revere and the Old North Church, North End.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The trail finishes, as it does, across the river in Charlestown at the
    <strong>Bunker Hill Monument</strong>. It is a 221-foot granite obelisk commemorating the battle of June 1775, the
    first major battle of the Revolution. The battle was actually fought mostly on Breed's Hill, where the monument
    stands, not on Bunker Hill next door, but the name stuck to everything anyway.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bunker-hill.webp'} alt="The tall granite Bunker Hill Monument obelisk at the top of a flight of steps." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Bunker Hill Monument, Charlestown.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'freedom-trail-sign.webp'} alt="A Freedom Trail information sign with a map of Boston, reading The Revolution Begins Here." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The end of the trail, or the beginning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We reached it at six in the evening.
    At its foot, the trail's last sign reads "The Revolution Begins Here", which feels like a strange thing to read at
    the end of it.
  </p>

  <h3>Vermont, briefly, and the birth of a mascot</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0804.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0804.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3: Boston to Rochester, via Vermont, Albany and Syracuse." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: Boston to Rochester, via Vermont, Albany and Syracuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Then the arc bent west. Leaving Boston, we cut northwest to clip the bottom corner of Vermont on I-91. At the
    Vermont welcome center in Guilford, just outside <strong>Brattleboro</strong>, a timber building with a giant wall
    map of the state, we made the most consequential purchase of the trip. In a glass case of local crafts and souvenirs,
    among the baskets, carvings and stuffed animals, sat a small toy hedgehog. (More on him later. There is a lot
    of him later.)
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vermont-map.webp'} alt="A large framed road map of Vermont on a wooden wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Vermont welcome center in Guilford.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vermont-case.webp'} alt="A glass display case of Vermont souvenirs and crafts on a wooden floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The case where it all began.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From there it was one of those days that looks unreasonable written down: <strong>Brattleboro → Albany → Syracuse
    → Rochester</strong>, more than three hundred miles with three real stops. It is the kind of day you only plan when
    you have never driven it before.
  </p>

  <h3>Albany</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Albany</strong> was the surprise. New York's state capital is not on most people's list of places to see,
    and it should be, if only for <strong>Empire State Plaza</strong>. The plaza was the pet project of Governor Nelson
    Rockefeller. It was built between 1965 and 1976 after, the story goes, a visiting princess was driven through
    Albany's run-down center and Rockefeller was embarrassed. He cleared some forty blocks of the old city and replaced
    them with a 1960s fever dream in white marble. A row of four identical office towers lines one side of a long
    reflecting pool. At the end rises the Corning Tower, for decades the tallest building in New York State outside
    New York City. And in the middle sits The Egg, a performing arts center shaped like a flying saucer balanced on its
    edge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'albany-egg.webp'} alt="The Egg, a concrete performing arts center shaped like a tilted bowl, beside a dark reflecting pool at Empire State Plaza." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Egg, Empire State Plaza.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'albany-cubes.webp'} alt="A rusted steel sculpture of interlocking cubes on the plaza, with the city and hills beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One of the plaza's modern sculptures.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Walking across it on a Sunday afternoon, you feel like the last person on Earth. The plaza is enormous, a
    quarter-mile of white marble and stone with almost no one on it. The towers are reflected upside down in the long
    black pools, and the wind carries the faint sound of the highway below. Clouds drift over the towers, and
    their shadows slide across the marble. There are no shops, no cafés, no street noise, just geometry, sky and the
    occasional state employee crossing to a car.
    It is strange, cold and completely compelling. The plaza is also an open-air museum: Rockefeller filled it with
    modern art, and sculptures sit around it like chess pieces, including a rusted steel cluster of interlocking cubes
    on the terrace. At the north end, the old <strong>New York State Capitol</strong> looks like it was airlifted in
    from the Loire Valley. It took over thirty years and five architects to build, finishing in 1899, and was one of the
    most expensive government buildings of its age. A giant "I ❤ NY" sign stands in front of it, for anyone who missed
    the point. From the plaza's edge you can look down over the highway interchange to the Hudson.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'albany-empire-plaza.webp'} alt="I love NY sign in front of the New York State Capitol at Empire State Plaza." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Albany: Empire State Plaza.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hedgehog-albany.webp'} alt="A small toy hedgehog next to a pineapple slush on a café table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The hedgehog's first day on the job, at a café in Loudonville, just north of Albany.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The hedgehog's first official outing was a café just north of the city, where he sat for his portrait next to a
    pineapple slush. This set a pattern. From then on, whenever there was a drink or a dessert on a table, the hedgehog
    was placed next to it and photographed. By the end of the trip he had an extensive portfolio.
  </p>

  <h3>Syracuse at golden hour</h3>
  <p>
    The day ended with a golden-hour walk across <strong>Syracuse University</strong>, which sits on a hill
    above the city, known simply as the Hill. In twenty minutes we passed a whole architecture textbook. The
    <strong>Hall of Languages</strong> (1873) was the university's first building, a pale stone Second Empire pile with
    mansard roofs. Crouse College (1889) is a dark Romanesque castle of red stone with a bell tower. Hendricks Chapel,
    domed and red-brick with a white portico, is the spiritual center of the campus. The red-brick turrets of the
    Tolley Building and the little Holden Observatory, from 1887, round out the set.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'syracuse-crouse.webp'} alt="Crouse College, a dark red-stone Romanesque building with a tall bell tower, on a grassy hill under a blue evening sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Crouse College (1889), in the low evening sun.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Every one of them was lit warm and low by the setting sun, with the city spread out below and the long shadows of
    the trees reaching across the lawns. It was the most beautiful light of the trip so far, and the campus was almost
    empty, students still away for the summer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Golden hour on a hilltop campus in late summer has a sound as well as a look: sprinklers ticking on the lawns,
    crickets starting up in the hedges, and almost no voices. The stone is warm to the touch when you lean on a wall.
    You walk slowly because there is nowhere to be, and because you know the light will be gone in twenty minutes.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'syracuse-hall-of-languages.webp'} alt="Syracuse University's Hall of Languages, a pale stone Second Empire building with towers, in evening sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hall of Languages (1873), Syracuse's first building.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'syracuse-hendricks.webp'} alt="Hendricks Chapel, a domed red-brick building with stone trim, in golden evening light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hendricks Chapel, in the last of the sun.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Downtown, the Soldiers' and Sailors' Monument in Clinton Square, where the Erie Canal once ran through the middle
    of the city, was the last thing we saw before the night's drive to a Hampton Inn in Henrietta, just south of Rochester.
  </p>

  <h3>Lake Ontario, and Niagara</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0805.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0805.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 4: Rochester to Pittsburgh, along two Great Lakes and past Niagara." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: Rochester to Pittsburgh, along two Great Lakes and past Niagara.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The next morning started on the sand at Rochester's <strong>Ontario Beach Park</strong>: a boardwalk, a red-brick
    building in a little roundabout garden, and a wide beach with almost nobody on it, Lake Ontario flat and pale to the
    horizon. If you didn't know it was a lake you'd never guess; there is no far shore. It was the first of the Great
    Lakes of the trip, and over the next four days we would see all of them but Superior.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rochester-pavilion.webp'} alt="A red-brick building with white trim behind a round flower garden at Ontario Beach Park." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ontario Beach Park, Rochester.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rochester-beach.webp'} alt="A wide empty sand beach and boardwalk on Lake Ontario under a hazy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lake Ontario, with nobody on the beach.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Next was the first jaw-drop of the trip, <strong>Niagara Falls</strong>, by way of <strong>Old Fort
    Niagara</strong>, where the Niagara River meets Lake Ontario. The fort's oldest building, the stone "French Castle",
    dates from 1726. The fort was held in turn by the French, the British and the Americans, and changed hands more
    than once, which tells you how much this stretch of river was worth.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'old-fort-niagara.webp'} alt="A shaded lawn with a lamppost and a large tree, and the Niagara River beyond a chain-link fence, with buildings on the far bank." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Niagara River from the grounds of Old Fort Niagara.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At the falls themselves, we walked Goat Island out to <strong>Terrapin Point</strong>, close enough to the
    Horseshoe Falls to get wet. Most of the Horseshoe is technically in Canada, but Terrapin Point puts you right at its
    American edge, where the river pours over the lip a few feet from the railing with a sound you feel in your chest.
    The mist rises so high that the Canadian skyline on the far bank comes and goes behind it, and the sun turns it into
    shifting columns of light. Far below, a tour boat full of people in ponchos nosed into the spray. The boats have
    been running into the mist at the foot of the falls since 1846.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'terrapin-mist.webp'} alt="The edge of the Horseshoe Falls at Terrapin Point, with mist rising and the Canadian skyline faint behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The edge of the Horseshoe Falls, from Terrapin Point.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'niagara-boat.webp'} alt="A tour boat in the churning water at the base of the falls, beneath the mist." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A tour boat heading into the spray.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Standing there, you hear Niagara before you understand it. The roar is low and constant, more felt than heard,
    and it never stops. The water near the edge is not white but a startling glassy green, moving so fast and so
    smoothly that it looks solid until it tips over the lip and shatters. The mist drifts over the railing in waves.
    One moment the sun is warm on your face, the next you are wet, and a few seconds later the mist lifts again. Your
    glasses fog, your phone screen is beaded with droplets, and everyone around you is laughing at the same
    thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    From the other side of the island, the American Falls and the Rainbow Bridge line up with the Skylon Tower in
    Canada beyond. It is a view that looks exactly like a postcard, because it is on most of them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'niagara-falls.webp'} alt="Niagara Falls seen from the American side with the Rainbow Bridge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The American Falls, from Niagara Falls State Park.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the way out, we said hello to the statue of <strong>Nikola Tesla</strong> on Goat Island. He sits on a rock,
    reading, in a little garden, looking calm for a man who spent his life fighting about electricity. The statue was a
    gift from Yugoslavia in 1976, and it is here for a reason. In 1895 the Adams Power Plant at Niagara became the first
    large hydroelectric plant to use Tesla's alternating-current system. It sent power to Buffalo, twenty miles away,
    and in doing so more or less settled the "war of the currents" against Edison's direct current. Every outlet in your
    house is, in a sense, descended from this river.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'niagara-tesla.webp'} alt="A bronze statue of Nikola Tesla seated on a rock, reading, surrounded by plants." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Nikola Tesla on Goat Island.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Buffalo, Erie and a sky on fire</h3>
  <p>
    Buffalo was next, for wings at the Anchor Bar, which is credited with inventing them in 1964. The
    story, which may even be true, is that the owner, Teressa Bellissimo, was faced with a delivery of chicken wings,
    then a cheap cut usually thrown into stock, and deep-fried them, tossed them in hot sauce and butter, and served them
    with celery and blue cheese from the bar's kitchen. They arrived exactly as the legend requires: a heaping platter,
    celery sticks standing up in the middle, cups of blue cheese on the side.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'buffalo-wings.webp'} alt="A platter of Buffalo wings, some sauced and some dry-rubbed, with celery sticks standing in the middle and cups of blue cheese." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wings, at the source.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On Niagara Square, Buffalo's Art Deco City Hall faces the McKinley Monument obelisk.
    Downtown, a sign painted on the side of a building on Main Street asks visitors to "Keep Buffalo a Secret", a wink
    from a city that has long been the punchline of weather jokes. The sky was gray when we walked Niagara Square, and it
    was raining by the time we drove out; the last photos of downtown are taken through a windshield beaded with rain.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'buffalo-city-hall.webp'} alt="Buffalo City Hall, a tall Art Deco tower with a colonnaded base, under a gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Buffalo City Hall, on Niagara Square.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'buffalo-secret.webp'} alt="A rainy Buffalo street with a church spire and a painted wall reading Keep Buffalo a Secret." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Keep Buffalo a Secret", on Main Street.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By the time we reached the beach at Presque Isle in <strong>Erie</strong>, Pennsylvania, it had cleared enough to
    look back across the bay at the city. Presque Isle ("almost an island" in French) is a sandy peninsula that curls
    seven miles out into Lake Erie and wraps around a natural harbor. That harbor is where Oliver Hazard Perry's fleet
    was built in 1813, before the Battle of Lake Erie. From the beach, the city across the water looks like a model
    railway town, with the Bicentennial Tower sticking up on the waterfront.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'erie-bay.webp'} alt="The Erie waterfront and Bicentennial Tower seen across choppy water from Presque Isle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Erie, across the bay from Presque Isle.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Presque Isle's beaches face the open lake, and on a windy afternoon after rain they look like the ocean: dark
    sand, a line of foam, small waves breaking one after another, and a horizon with nothing on it. The water is
    fresh, though, and it smells of nothing at all, which is the giveaway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'erie-presque-isle-beach.webp'} alt="A wide dark-sand beach at Presque Isle with small waves, rock breakwaters offshore and a cloudy sky over Lake Erie." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The beach at Presque Isle, Lake Erie.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then came a long run south on I-79 toward Pittsburgh while the sky put on a show. The storms that had soaked Buffalo
    had moved east, and as the sun went down in the west it lit them from the side: towering anvil clouds turned gold and
    then salmon-pink above the dark Pennsylvania woods. We photographed them through the windshield again and again,
    past Meadville and on into the dusk, because each one looked better than the last.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a particular feeling to a long interstate drive at the end of a full day. The radio is low, nobody has
    said anything for twenty minutes, and the road unspools ahead in long, gentle curves through the trees. The light
    goes from gold to pink to blue, and the cloud towers change color every time you glance out the side window.
    The mile markers count down to the next city. You are tired, and it is one of the best hours of the trip.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i79-sunset.webp'} alt="Sunset-lit clouds over an interstate highway in Pennsylvania." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>I-79 in Crawford County, Pennsylvania, around 8 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Pittsburgh</h3>
  <p>
    The Rust Belt was its own chapter. We had spent the night at a Hampton Inn in Green Tree, just outside <strong>Pittsburgh</strong>, and in the city we went up Mount Washington, to Grandview Avenue
    and the top of the Monongahela Incline, for the view of the three rivers. Downtown sits on its wedge of land where
    the Allegheny and the Monongahela meet to form the Ohio, stitched to everything else by bridges. Pittsburgh is said
    to have more bridges than any other city in the world. From up here you can see why it was built where it was:
    whoever held the Point held the gateway to the whole Ohio valley, which is why the French and the British fought
    over it in the 1750s.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0806.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0806.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 5: Pittsburgh, Wheeling and Columbus, ending outside Cleveland." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 5: Pittsburgh, Wheeling and Columbus, ending outside Cleveland.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pittsburgh.webp'} alt="Downtown Pittsburgh skyline and rivers from a hillside overlook." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pittsburgh from Grandview Avenue on Mount Washington.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Grandview Avenue runs along the very edge of the bluff, with a string of overlooks cantilevered out over the
    drop. You walk from one to the next, and at each one the city shifts slightly: here the stadiums on the North
    Shore, there the fountain at the Point, then the glass spires of PPG Place glinting like a crystal castle. That day the sky was full of bright, fast-moving clouds, and their shadows
    swept across downtown so the city flickered between light and shade as you watched.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Monongahela Incline has been hauling people up and down this slope since 1870, and it is the oldest
    continuously operating funicular in the country. It was built for the steelworkers who lived on top of the hill
    and worked in the mills along the river, back when the city's air was so thick with smoke that streetlights burned
    at noon. Its upper station has a bay window where people line up just to stand and look at a city that is now,
    in the clear air, unrecognizable from those days.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pittsburgh-incline-window.webp'} alt="Downtown Pittsburgh and a river bridge framed by a dark window at the top of the Monongahela Incline." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from the incline's upper station.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pittsburgh-incline-track.webp'} alt="Looking down the steep incline track through the paned window of the upper station, with a car waiting at the platform." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Down the track, through the station window.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then we crossed town to Oakland, Pittsburgh's university district. The <strong>Cathedral of Learning</strong> at
    Pitt is forty-two stories and 535 feet of limestone Gothic, the tallest educational building in the Western
    Hemisphere. It is a university building that looks like a church that kept growing. Inside, its Nationality Rooms are
    classrooms each designed in the style of a different country, built by the city's immigrant communities. Next to it,
    Heinz Memorial Chapel's spire was wrapped in scaffolding.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pitt-cathedral.webp'} alt="The Cathedral of Learning, a very tall limestone Gothic tower, against a blue sky with clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pitt's Cathedral of Learning.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pitt-heinz.webp'} alt="Heinz Memorial Chapel with its spire wrapped in scaffolding, seen across a hedge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Heinz Memorial Chapel, under repair.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Across Forbes Avenue stands the Carnegie Museum and Music Hall, a Beaux-Arts palace that Andrew Carnegie
    gave the city in 1895. Outside it, a life-size bronze Diplodocus, Dippy, grazes on the lawn by Forbes Avenue.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pitt-carnegie.webp'} alt="The Beaux-Arts facade of the Carnegie Museum and Music Hall in Pittsburgh." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Carnegie Museum and Music Hall.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pitt-dippy.webp'} alt="A life-size bronze Diplodocus statue on a hedge-lined lawn in front of the Carnegie Museum, under a cloudy blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dippy, outside the Carnegie Museum.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few blocks on is the Soldiers and Sailors Memorial, and then <strong>Carnegie Mellon</strong>,
    whose glassy Cohon University Center was quiet in the August heat. Carnegie's money built a remarkable share of this
    neighborhood; Pittsburgh's steel paid for it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'cmu.webp'} alt="The modern glass entrance of the Cohon University Center at Carnegie Mellon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Carnegie Mellon's Cohon University Center.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The afternoon ended with a matcha break for the hedgehog in Squirrel
    Hill: an iced matcha and a soft-serve sundae, both bright green, with the hedgehog between them looking pleased.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hedgehog-pittsburgh.webp'} alt="The toy hedgehog sitting between two matcha desserts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Matcha break in Squirrel Hill.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Wheeling and Columbus</h3>
  <p>
    Then came <strong>Wheeling, West Virginia</strong>, which deserves more than a footnote, because it is the
    purest example of what this trip was. It was the only place we stopped in West Virginia. We didn't plan it as a
    destination; it just happened to sit on the road between Pittsburgh and Columbus, where I-70 crosses the narrow
    northern panhandle of the state, a sliver of West Virginia barely fifteen miles wide squeezed between
    Pennsylvania and Ohio. You could cross the whole state here in a quarter of an hour without stopping. We stopped.
  </p>
  <p>
    It was worth it. Wheeling sits on the Ohio River where the old National Road, the country's first federally
    built highway, crossed it, and in the nineteenth century it was a rich and important city, a gateway to the West
    where travelers left the road for the river. Its suspension bridge was the longest in the world when it opened in
    1849. Downtown still has the bones of that wealth: whole blocks of handsome Victorian brick with arched windows,
    heavy cornices and cast-iron storefronts, built for a city three times the size. On the afternoon we came through,
    Main Street had just been rebuilt, with fresh sidewalks, new trees in their planters, and orange traffic cones
    still standing in the road. Half an hour, a walk around the block, and one fridge magnet later, West Virginia
    was on the fridge, and we were back on I-70 heading west. That is the cursory road trip in a nutshell: you
    don't get to know a place, but you do get to say hello to it, and sometimes that is enough.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wheeling.webp'} alt="A three-story Victorian brick commercial block with arched windows and cast-iron storefronts on a freshly rebuilt street in downtown Wheeling." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Wheeling, the trip's only stop in West Virginia.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wheeling-clock.webp'} alt="A green street clock reading Welcome Wheeling on a new sidewalk, with an old brick wall and a painted pillar behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Wheeling street clock.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By dusk we were in <strong>Columbus</strong>, Ohio's capital. We walked past the <strong>Ohio
    Statehouse</strong> under a heavy gray sky. It is a capitol with a flat-topped drum where the dome should be: the
    building took over twenty years to finish, the money and the arguments ran out, and the dome was never built.
    Purists consider it one of the finest Greek Revival buildings in the country for exactly that reason. William
    McKinley, Ohio's governor before he was president, stands on a plinth out front.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'columbus-statehouse.webp'} alt="The Ohio Statehouse, a Greek Revival building with a flat-topped drum, and the McKinley monument at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Ohio Statehouse and the McKinley monument, at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'osu-night.webp'} alt="A modern building with a glowing red glass facade and skybridge on the Ohio State campus at nightfall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Ohio State campus at nightfall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then we took a stroll up Neil Avenue onto the <strong>Ohio State</strong> campus as the lights came on, ten minutes
    of lit windows and empty paths at one of the largest universities in the country. Dinner was at the Hofbräuhaus,
    a branch of Munich's famous beer hall: sausages, sauerkraut, mashed potatoes and a pork knuckle, about as far from
    Denver ramen as a week can get. After that came a night drive to the Embassy Suites in Beachwood, just outside <strong>Cleveland</strong>. Which
    brings us back to the hedgehog.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'columbus-hofbrauhaus.webp'} alt="A pork knuckle with sauerkraut and mashed potatoes, and a plate of sausages behind it, on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pork knuckle at the Hofbräuhaus, Columbus.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Cleveland, and the hedgehog's first escape</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0807.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0807.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 6: Cleveland, twice, then Detroit." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 6: Cleveland, twice, then Detroit.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We checked out the next morning and walked around Case Western Reserve. There was a white abstract sculpture on the
    lawn and, behind it, Frank Gehry's Peter B. Lewis Building, home of the business school. Its brick walls bulge and
    lean, and great sheets of stainless steel pour off the roof like a building that is melting from the top down. Up
    close it is even stranger: the brick curves inward over the entrance, the windows are set at slightly wrong
    angles, and the steel overhangs look like the folds of a giant napkin.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cwru-sculpture.webp'} alt="A white abstract sculpture on a lawn at Case Western Reserve, with Gehry's brick building behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Case Western Reserve, with Gehry behind the trees.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cwru-gehry.webp'} alt="Close-up of Frank Gehry's Peter B. Lewis Building: curving brick walls under a swooping steel overhang." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gehry's Peter B. Lewis Building, up close.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Next door, in University Circle, we spent about an hour inside the Cleveland History Center and the Cleveland
    Museum of Art. Then we went downtown, under the Playhouse Square arch, and into the <strong>Arcade</strong>, an
    1890 shopping arcade with a glass roof.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cleveland-playhouse-square.webp'} alt="An ornate Playhouse Square arch over Euclid Avenue, with theater signs and downtown towers behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Playhouse Square, on Euclid Avenue.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cleveland-arcade.webp'} alt="The empty interior of the Cleveland Arcade: a long glass roof over tiers of balconies and a mosaic floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Arcade, from 1890.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Somewhere along the way we realized the hedgehog wasn't with us. He was still in Beachwood, where we had started
    the morning. So my wife stayed in the Arcade while I drove back, retrieved one (1) toy hedgehog, and came back to
    pick her up as if nothing had happened. That is why we were still in Cleveland at three in the afternoon.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then we went down to the lakefront at North Coast Harbor together. The "Cleveland" script sign sits in front of the
    white geometry of the <strong>Rock &amp; Roll Hall of Fame</strong>, I. M. Pei's glass pyramid and tilted boxes,
    which opened in 1995 and is a clear cousin of his pyramid at the Louvre. Along the
    harbor walk, the Browns' stadium sits right on the lake, and Key Tower, the tallest building in Ohio, rises
    over downtown. We didn't tour the Hall of Fame, but we did get a magnet from its gift shop. The sky over all of it
    was a solid ceiling of dark cloud, clearly thinking about a storm.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cleveland.webp'} alt="The Cleveland script sign on the lakefront with the downtown skyline." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cleveland's North Coast Harbor, the last stop of the day.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cleveland-lakefront.webp'} alt="The brick harbor walk at North Coast Harbor under heavy clouds, with the stadium in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The harbor walk, under a storm ceiling.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Detroit</h3>
  <p>
    By the time we reached <strong>Detroit</strong> the storm had blown off and the sky was a clean, hard blue. From
    the riverfront you look straight across the water at Canada: the skyline of Windsor, Ontario, which from Detroit is
    due <em>south</em>. It is a fact that wins bar bets. The Detroit River bends here so that, for a stretch, Canada lies
    south of the United States.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'detroit-windsor.webp'} alt="The Windsor, Ontario skyline across the Detroit River, seen from a Detroit riverfront parking lot." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Canada, to the south: Windsor across the Detroit River.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Detroit's riverfront in the early evening is wide, quiet and full of light. The river is broad and fast, the
    color of slate, and freighters pass slowly between the two countries. Across it, Windsor's towers
    catch the sun, and gulls wheel over the water. Behind you, the downtown towers stand in long shadows, many of
    them grand buildings from the 1920s, when this was one of the richest cities in the world.
  </p>
  <p>
    Downtown is dominated by the <strong>Renaissance Center</strong>, a cluster of dark glass cylinders that is very
    much a 1970s idea of the future. It was designed by John Portman and opened in 1977, built with the hope that a
    gleaming new complex would pull the city back from its long decline. It has been General Motors'
    headquarters since 1996. It still looks like a set from a science-fiction film, and in the evening sun its
    towers turned almost black against the sky.
  </p>
  <p>
    At Hart Plaza, by the river, stands <em>Transcending</em>, the Michigan Labor Legacy Landmark: a giant steel ring,
    sixty-three feet tall, broken at the top. It honors the labor movement that was born in this city's auto plants.
    The ring is left open on purpose, because the work is unfinished. It stands amid flags on a plaza that, on a
    Wednesday evening in August, was almost empty.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'detroit.webp'} alt="The Renaissance Center towers in downtown Detroit." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Detroit: the Renaissance Center, from Randolph Street.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'detroit-transcending.webp'} alt="Transcending, a tall broken steel ring honoring labor, at Hart Plaza in Detroit, with flags behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Transcending</em>, the Michigan Labor Legacy Landmark at Hart Plaza, Detroit.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Lansing and Michigan City</h3>
  <p>
    We spent that night at a Hampton Inn in Howell, between Detroit and Lansing. From there the Great Lakes reeled us in. In <strong>Lansing</strong> at midday, the Michigan State Capitol looked
    small and perfect under a big sky streaked with cirrus. It has a Colorado connection: its architect, Elijah E.
    Myers, also designed the Colorado State Capitol we had walked past in Denver ten days earlier, and the family
    resemblance between the two is obvious once you know to look. Myers designed more state capitols than any other
    architect, and it shows in the formula: a dome on a drum, a portico and two wings.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0808.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0808.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 7: across Michigan to Chicago, and a night in Kenosha." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 7: across Michigan to Chicago, and a night in Kenosha.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    After that we stopped in <strong>Michigan City</strong>, Indiana, on the southern shore of Lake Michigan, where
    Franklin Street is lined with public sculptures: painted metal flowers, abstract forms, and a dark pedestal topped
    with a sailing ship, standing beside a red-brick church steeple.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lansing.webp'} alt="The Michigan State Capitol in Lansing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lansing: the Michigan State Capitol.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'michigan-city.webp'} alt="A public sculpture of a sailing ship atop a dark pedestal on a street in Michigan City, Indiana." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Public art on Franklin Street, Michigan City.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Chicago, the first evening</h3>
  <p>
    And then came <strong>Chicago</strong>, which we liked so much that we photographed it in three separate sessions:
    arriving in the late afternoon, a night on the way back, and a whole morning downtown. We came in up South Michigan
    Avenue, through the old Motor Row and Printer's Row, with the skyline building ahead of us block by block and Grant
    Park opening up on the right.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-chicago-0808.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-chicago-0808.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Chicago, August 8: the first evening on foot, ending at the top of the Hancock." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Chicago, August 8: the first evening on foot, ending at the top of the Hancock.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Chicago announces itself. You come up the avenue and the buildings get taller and closer together until you are
    walking in a canyon: limestone on one side, glass on the other. The scale of the place is the first thing you feel. The sidewalks are wide,
    the buildings are huge, and everything is solid and confident in a way that newer cities aren't.
    We spent the first evening on foot. The Magnificent Mile, the stretch of Michigan Avenue north of the river, has
    the old <strong>Water Tower</strong> of 1869, which survived the Great Fire of 1871. It sits like a little limestone
    sandcastle among the skyscrapers, the only public building in the burned district left standing. Nearby is the
    tapering black X-braced Hancock tower.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-water-tower.webp'} alt="The Gothic-style limestone Chicago Water Tower among glass skyscrapers, with a yellow taxi passing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Water Tower, a survivor of 1871.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-hancock.webp'} alt="The tapering black Hancock tower with its X-bracing, seen from street level." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hancock tower.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The avenue also has a 24-hour "bakery ATM" that vends cakes through a pink
    glass door, and, all along it, giant guitar and banjo sculptures under banners reading "Chicago Welcomes DNC 2024".
    The Democratic National Convention was eleven days away, and the city was already dressed for it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-bakery-atm.webp'} alt="A pink vending machine labeled 24HR bakery ATM, filled with boxed cakes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A 24-hour bakery ATM.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-dnc-banjo.webp'} alt="A giant banjo sculpture on Michigan Avenue with Chicago Welcomes DNC 2024 banners." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Michigan Avenue, eleven days before the convention.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On the same avenue, the hedgehog posed with an iced latte and a slice of tiramisu by the window of the Starbucks
    Reserve Roastery.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hedgehog-chicago.webp'} alt="The toy hedgehog beside an iced latte, a slice of tiramisu and a mushroom flatbread at a window table above Michigan Avenue." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The hedgehog on Michigan Avenue.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At the river, the <strong>Wrigley Building</strong> and <strong>Tribune Tower</strong> face each other across
    Michigan Avenue like two wedding cakes. The Wrigley is white terra-cotta with a clock tower modeled on the Giralda in
    Seville. The Tribune is Gothic, with a crown of flying buttresses, and it won one of the most famous architecture
    competitions ever held, in 1922. Its walls are studded with fragments of famous buildings from around the world,
    from the Great Wall to the Parthenon, brought back by the newspaper's correspondents.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-wrigley-tribune.webp'} alt="The white Wrigley Building clock tower and the Gothic Tribune Tower across Michigan Avenue at the river, under a gray evening sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Wrigley Building (left) and Tribune Tower, on Michigan Avenue.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The Michigan Avenue bridge is where all of Chicago seems to converge on a summer evening. Crowds line
    the railings, the bridge houses at its corners are carved with scenes from the city's history, and
    the river opens up in both directions between walls of towers.
    Below the bridge, the river was a traffic jam of architecture-tour boats, each packed with people looking up. The
    river itself is a feat of engineering: in 1900 the city reversed its flow, so that it carries sewage away from Lake
    Michigan, the drinking-water supply, instead of into it. We followed it east along Wacker Drive past the glass
    tower that steps back in tiers to line up with the heights of its neighbors, and past the wavy blue-glass St. Regis
    by Jeanne Gang, then the third-tallest building in the city.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-river.webp'} alt="The Chicago River lined with skyscrapers, with a tour boat below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Chicago River from Streeterville, first evening.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-tour-boat.webp'} alt="A crowded architecture tour boat on the Chicago River below a tall glass tower." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The architecture tour, from above.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then we went up the Hancock to the <strong>360 Chicago</strong> observation deck, a thousand feet up on the
    ninety-fourth floor. The building was designed by Bruce Graham and the structural engineer Fazlur Khan of Skidmore,
    Owings &amp; Merrill. The X-braces on its outside are not decoration: they make the whole facade into a stiff tube,
    which let them build a hundred stories with far less steel than usual. That idea was a turning point in how
    supertall buildings are made.
  </p>
  <p>
    The museum floor glows with neon maps of the city's neighborhoods, and one window carries the slogan "My Block, My
    Hood, My City" in big black letters over the view.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-360-neon.webp'} alt="A dark museum wall with a glowing neon outline map of Chicago's neighborhoods." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Neon neighborhoods at 360 Chicago.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-my-block.webp'} alt="Large black letters reading My Block My Hood My City on an observation window above the city." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"My Block, My Hood, My City".</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We watched dusk fall from the top. The lakeshore curves north in a
    perfect arc, Lake Shore Drive tracing it, and Navy Pier pokes out into the lake.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-from-above.webp'} alt="Chicago's lakefront and Lake Shore Drive at dusk from an observation deck." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lakefront at dusk, from 360 Chicago.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-navy-pier-dusk.webp'} alt="Navy Pier lit up at dusk from high above, its Ferris wheel glowing, with boats on the dark lake." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Navy Pier lights up.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Westward, the grid of the city switched on, light by light, all the way to the horizon. Chicago is famously flat,
    and from up here it is obvious: the grid simply keeps going until it dissolves into the haze.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-night-grid.webp'} alt="Downtown Chicago at night from high above, the street grid lit up to the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The grid switches on.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Wisconsin, and back</h3>
  <p>
    That night we slept just over the Wisconsin line, at a Hampton Inn in Kenosha. The next day we looped north to
    <strong>Milwaukee</strong>, where a modern streetcar runs downtown under its overhead wires and where we stocked up on cheese.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0809.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0809.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 8: a loop through Milwaukee and Madison, back to Chicago for the night." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 8: a loop through Milwaukee and Madison, back to Chicago for the night.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'milwaukee-public-market.webp'} alt="The Milwaukee Public Market, a long building with a slatted canopy, under streetcar wires, with an old black truck parked out front and a red sculpture at the corner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Milwaukee Public Market, under the streetcar wires.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From there we went on to <strong>Madison</strong>. Madison sits on a narrow isthmus between two lakes, Mendota and
    Monona. Its white granite capitol stands at the top of the isthmus, and the city has long limited the height of
    buildings around it so that every street seems to aim at its dome.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'madison.webp'} alt="The Wisconsin State Capitol in Madison under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Madison: the Wisconsin State Capitol, from Mansion Hill.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Back in Chicago we found Hunan food in Chinatown, and we got to Hyde Park in time to see a Gothic chapel lit up at
    dusk on the University of Chicago campus, its stone glowing gold from floodlights in the trees. That night we slept
    at Sable at Navy Pier (it's in the <a href="/misc/hotels">hotels post</a>). The window that night
    looked back across the harbor at the whole lit-up skyline. In the morning, the same window showed white cruise boats
    tied up below and the lake turned a ridiculous shade of blue.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'uchicago-chapel.webp'} alt="A Gothic stone chapel at the University of Chicago, lit gold by floodlights at dusk, framed by a tree." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Gothic chapel at the University of Chicago, at dusk.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Chicago, the last morning</h3>
  <p>
    The last morning was the best weather of the trip. Navy Pier was built in 1916 as a shipping pier and has been,
    over the years, a naval training center, a university campus, and finally an amusement pier. At nine in the morning
    it was nearly empty. From its end, the whole skyline stands in a row across the water, from Lake Point Tower to
    the towers of the Loop.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-chicago-0810.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-chicago-0810.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Chicago, August 10: Navy Pier, the river, the Architecture Center, and lunch in the West Loop." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Chicago, August 10: Navy Pier, the river, the Architecture Center, and lunch in the West Loop.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-skyline.webp'} alt="The Chicago skyline across Lake Michigan on a clear morning." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The skyline from the lake, the morning we left.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Mornings on the lake are a different city from evenings downtown. The air is cool and clean, the water
    glitters, joggers and cyclists stream along the lakefront path, and the skyline looks freshly washed, every
    window catching the low sun from the east.
  </p>
  <p>
    We walked back through Streeterville and down to the river, where the boats were already lining up, and spent an
    hour at the <strong>Chicago Architecture Center</strong>. Its scale model of downtown, with a film playing on a big
    screen behind it, is worth the trip on its own. It contains thousands of buildings, and you can stand over it and
    find the street you just walked down.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-city-model.webp'} alt="A large scale model of downtown Chicago at the Chicago Architecture Center." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The city model at the Chicago Architecture Center.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Upstairs, a gallery of skyscraper portraits included a nod to home: Los Angeles's <strong>Eastern Columbia
    Building</strong>, the turquoise-and-gold Art Deco tower on Broadway, built in 1930 as a clothing store and now
    lofts. There was also a black-and-gold model of the <strong>Carbide &amp; Carbon Building</strong> on Michigan
    Avenue. It was designed in 1929 by the sons of Daniel Burnham, and legend says it was meant to look like a
    champagne bottle, with dark green terra-cotta for the glass and gold leaf for the foil at the top.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cac-eastern-columbia.webp'} alt="A gallery panel showing the turquoise Art Deco Eastern Columbia Building in Los Angeles." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A nod to home: LA's Eastern Columbia Building.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cac-carbide.webp'} alt="A black-and-gold model of the Carbide and Carbon Building on a white plinth in a gallery." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Carbide &amp; Carbon Building, in miniature.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Finally, a row of
    white models of the world's tallest towers stood in front of a window that looks straight out at the real Wrigley
    Building, as if the models and the city were having a conversation.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cac-towers.webp'} alt="White models of supertall towers in front of a window looking out at the Wrigley Building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Models of supertall towers, and the real city outside.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Back at the pier, we rode the <strong>Centennial Wheel</strong> at Navy Pier, two hundred feet up. Below were the
    carousel and the pier's long roofs; to one side stood Lake Point Tower, the only skyscraper east of Lake Shore
    Drive; and the whole skyline fit in a single frame. The original Ferris wheel was invented for Chicago's 1893
    World's Fair as the city's answer to the Eiffel Tower, so a Ferris wheel here is a kind of homecoming.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'navy-pier.webp'} alt="The Navy Pier Ferris wheel against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Navy Pier, last morning.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'centennial-wheel-view.webp'} alt="View down onto Navy Pier, its carousel and the Chicago skyline from the top of the Centennial Wheel." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Navy Pier from the top of the Centennial Wheel.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And finally, lunch, at McDonald's headquarters in the West Loop. The company moved its head office here in
    2018, into the old meatpacking district of Fulton Market, now a neighborhood of tech offices and restaurants. The
    building houses <strong>Hamburger University</strong>, the company's training center for managers, founded in 1961
    in a restaurant basement, and yes, it really is called that. But the reason to come is on the ground floor. The
    McDonald's there serves a rotating selection of items from its menus around the world, alongside the usual
    American ones. You can't get them anywhere else in the country, and for one lunch you can order across
    continents from the same counter. I ordered from the Hong Kong menu and from a European country's menu, which is
    as close as this trip came to leaving the United States. It was a fittingly strange last meal for the city where
    the modern McDonald's began: Ray Kroc's first franchise opened in Des Plaines, just outside Chicago, in 1955.
    If the trip had a capital, it was Chicago.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hamburger-university.webp'} alt="The glass entrance of Hamburger University at McDonald's headquarters in Chicago." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>McDonald's headquarters and Hamburger University, West Loop.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Act II: The teleport</h2>
  <p>
    Here's where we cheated, and I'd do it again. The middle of the country is vast, our vacation days were not, and we
    wanted to end somewhere warm and improbable. So we turned in the car and flew from <strong>Chicago to
    Orlando</strong>, connecting through Atlanta without leaving the terminal, and stayed two nights in Orlando, doing
    the exact opposite of a road trip: standing in one place, riding things, and having a wonderful time.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-orlando.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-orlando.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 9 to 11: Orlando, from the airport to Universal, the Orlando Eye and SeaWorld." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 9 to 11: Orlando, from the airport to Universal, the Orlando Eye and SeaWorld.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Stepping out of the airport in Orlando after Chicago is like walking into a sauna fully dressed. The air in
    August is hot, thick and wet, the sky is a hazy white-blue, and by midday the clouds start to pile up into
    towers that will become thunderstorms by three o'clock. Everything is green and growing, the palms rattle in a
    warm wind, and every building is air-conditioned to the temperature of a refrigerator, so your sunglasses fog up
    every time you walk outside.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first day was <strong>Universal Studios</strong>. Optimus Prime towers over the entrance to Transformers: The
    Ride 3D, which had a twenty-minute wait, practically nothing for August.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-transformers.webp'} alt="A giant Optimus Prime figure above the entrance to Transformers: The Ride 3D at Universal Studios Florida." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Universal Studios: Transformers, twenty-minute wait.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Across the park's central lagoon,
    the Simpsons' <strong>Springfield</strong> is built as a walk-through version of the cartoon town, and it is
    funnier than it has any right to be. Jebediah Springfield, the town's founder, stands on his plinth in his coonskin
    hat. Chief Wiggum holds a donut beside his squad car. The Lard Lad Donuts statue looms over the street, and a
    wall-sized mural shows what seems like every character the show has ever had, hundreds of yellow faces in a crowd.
    We ate at Krusty Burger, had a drink at Flaming Moe's, and came out of the Kwik-E-Mart with souvenirs.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-jebediah.webp'} alt="A statue of Jebediah Springfield from The Simpsons on a stone plinth at Universal Studios." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Jebediah Springfield, founder.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-lard-lad.webp'} alt="The giant Lard Lad Donuts statue holding up a huge donut, with pigeons perched on top, in front of a donut stand at Universal's Springfield." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lard Lad, with pigeons.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the afternoon came the classic Florida summer rain shower, the kind that empties the paths in thirty seconds
    and is gone in twenty minutes. It left the pavement shining and the air even thicker than before. After it passed, we wandered into the new
    <strong>DreamWorks Land</strong>, which had opened only that summer, and right next to it the E.T. Adventure, one of
    the last rides still running from the park's opening day in 1990, under a sky that couldn't decide.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-wiggum.webp'} alt="A figure of Chief Wiggum holding a donut and coffee beside his squad car at Universal's Springfield." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Chief Wiggum, on duty.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-dreamworks.webp'} alt="The DreamWorks Land entrance sign and the E.T. Adventure building after rain at Universal Studios." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>DreamWorks Land and E.T., after the rain.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The second day was <strong>SeaWorld</strong>, by way of the Orlando Eye on International Drive in the morning.
    It is a four-hundred-foot observation wheel, its big white rim framed by palm trees against storm clouds building up
    for the afternoon. My camera roll is nearly silent about SeaWorld itself, which says something about how busy the
    day was; my wife's has a handful of videos from the afternoon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'orlando-eye.webp'} alt="The Orlando Eye Ferris wheel framed by palm trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Orlando Eye on International Drive, the morning of the SeaWorld day.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Act III: The end of the road</h2>
  <p>
    After SeaWorld we drove south into the humidity. Around eight, near Hollywood, the Seminole Hard Rock's
    <strong>Guitar Hotel</strong> appeared beside the highway, its glass lit in stripes against a pink sky. We spent
    the night near <strong>Fort Lauderdale</strong>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'guitar-hotel-hollywood.webp'} alt="A hotel tower shaped like a giant electric guitar, its glass lit in colored stripes, rising above trees and streetlights against a pink and blue dusk sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Guitar Hotel from the highway, August 12, about 8 pm. My wife's photo.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The next morning took us through <strong>Miami Beach</strong> and across the causeway to downtown <strong>Miami</strong>,
    where a statue of Simón Bolívar stands in the heat. Then we went out onto the <strong>Overseas Highway</strong>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-keys.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-keys.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 11 and 12: south from Orlando, through Miami and down the Overseas Highway." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 11 and 12: south from Orlando, through Miami and down the Overseas Highway.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The Overseas Highway is the ribbon of US-1 that island-hops for 113 miles across the ocean, through Key Largo and
    down the chain of the Florida Keys to <strong>Key West</strong>. It has forty-two bridges, the longest of them the
    Seven Mile Bridge. Much of it is built on the bones of Henry Flagler's Overseas Railroad, an extravagant
    engineering project finished in 1912 that was wrecked by the Labor Day hurricane of 1935. The state bought what was
    left and turned the railbed into a road. For long stretches there is water on both sides and nothing else: the Gulf
    and Florida Bay on the right, the Atlantic on the left, both the same unreal turquoise, with the old railway
    bridges running alongside, rusting and broken.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'overseas-highway.webp'} alt="Open water stretching to a flat horizon beyond a concrete bridge rail, under a pale sky with low clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>On the Overseas Highway: a bridge rail, then nothing but water.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>
    Driving it is like nothing else on the trip. After Key Largo the land narrows until the islands are just wide
    enough for the road, a few palm trees and a bait shop, and then even that falls away and you are on a bridge with
    nothing but water to the horizon on either side. The color of the water changes with the depth and the light:
    pale jade over the sandbanks, deep blue in the channels, almost white where the sun hits it. Clouds float low and flat-bottomed over the sea, each one casting its own patch
    of shadow on the water. If ever a road called for the windows down, it is this one: the air smells of salt and
    warm asphalt. The mile markers count down from over a hundred toward zero, and zero is Key West.
  </p>

  <h3>Key West</h3>
  <p>
    We stopped for croquetas on Stock Island just before the last bridge, a plate of golden fried rolls with a cup of
    sauce, and reached the end of the road at six in the evening. We had started near the top of the East Coast and
    driven, more or less, to the bottom of it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-keywest.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-keywest.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Key West, August 13 and 14." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Key West, August 13 and 14.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'stock-island-croquetas.webp'} alt="A plate of croquetas with a small bowl of dipping sauce on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Croquetas on Stock Island.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    There's a buoy there, striped red, black and yellow and painted "90 miles to Cuba", that marks the southernmost
    point of the continental U.S. It isn't actually a buoy, and it isn't technically the southernmost point: that is a
    little further along, on a navy base you aren't allowed into. It is a giant concrete marker the city put up in 1983
    after people kept stealing the signs. None of that matters. We took the photo. Everyone takes the photo. The
    line to do it can run half an hour.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'southernmost-point.webp'} alt="The Southernmost Point buoy in Key West: 90 miles to Cuba." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>90 miles to Cuba.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Key West itself feels like a small town that happens to be on the edge of the world. The streets are lined with
    wooden houses with tin roofs and wide porches, shaded by huge trees; chickens wander freely across the sidewalks;
    and bicycles outnumber cars. The heat softens toward evening, the light turns golden, and the whole town
    starts drifting toward the water.
    Then we went on to <strong>Mallory Square</strong> on the harbor, Key West's traditional sunset spot, where every
    evening a crowd gathers with street performers to applaud the sun going down. The flags were snapping in the wind,
    and the sky over the Gulf was going gray and gold.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mallory-square.webp'} alt="Silhouetted flags and a pavilion at Mallory Square in Key West against an evening sky, with a bicycle in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mallory Square at sunset.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last morning was a slow one. We drove along the beach on South Roosevelt Boulevard and then went to the
    <strong>Ernest Hemingway Home</strong>, a Spanish Colonial house where Hemingway lived through most of the 1930s
    and wrote some of his best-known work. It is, functionally, a cat sanctuary with a famous author's furniture in it.
    Around sixty cats live there, many of them six-toed descendants of a polydactyl cat named Snow White that a ship's
    captain gave Hemingway.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hemingway-porch-cat.webp'} alt="A white-and-tabby cat with green eyes walking toward the camera on a green-painted porch beside a white wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Hemingway cat on the porch.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hemingway-garden-cat.webp'} alt="A fluffy tabby cat asleep in a garden bed of dry leaves against a white wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Another, asleep in the garden.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Inside, the house is cool and dim after the glare of the street, with high ceilings, tall windows and
    dark carved Spanish furniture. Out the windows the garden is a tangle of palms and tropical
    plants, and somewhere beyond it is the brick wall Hemingway built around the property to keep out curious
    visitors. Now the visitors are let in, and it is the cats who seem to be in charge.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cats sleep wherever they like, which is mostly on the exhibits. One was curled up on Hemingway's own white
    bedspread behind a velvet rope. Another was asleep in a carved wooden chair, roped off, beneath a sign politely
    asking visitors not to sit on the furniture. The sign is clearly meant for humans only.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'key-west-cat.webp'} alt="A black cat asleep on an antique bed at the Hemingway House." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A resident of the Ernest Hemingway Home, off duty.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hemingway-chair-cat.webp'} alt="A tabby cat asleep on a carved wooden chair behind a rope, under a sign asking visitors not to sit on the furniture." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sign is for humans.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lunch was a lobster roll at the airport, eaten from a cardboard box, which is a perfectly good way to leave Key
    West.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'lobster-roll.webp'} alt="A lobster roll in a takeout box." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Last lunch in Key West, at the airport.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>An afternoon in Atlanta</h3>
  <p>
    Getting home had one last surprise. We connected through Atlanta again, but this layover ran long enough to do
    something slightly unhinged. Atlanta's airport, one of the busiest in the world, has its own MARTA station, and the
    train runs to downtown in about twenty minutes. So we rode <strong>MARTA</strong> into the city and wandered
    around Five Points, the historic center where the city's first streets met.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-atlanta.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-atlanta.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 13: the Atlanta layover, by MARTA to Five Points and back." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 13: the Atlanta layover, by MARTA to Five Points and back.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Coming up out of a MARTA station into downtown Atlanta at six in the evening is a strange experience after an
    airport. Half an hour ago you were in a terminal full of people; now you are standing in a quiet canyon of office
    towers in the slanting late sun, with streetcar wires overhead, construction fences along the curbs, and the
    whole city to yourself. It feels like getting away with something.
    We passed the <strong>Henry W. Grady</strong> monument, a statue from 1891 of the newspaper editor who promoted the
    idea of a "New South" after the Civil War. From there we walked past downtown's towers to an overpass and the folded
    steel-and-glass petals of <strong>Mercedes-Benz Stadium</strong>, with construction cranes still working around it.
    The stadium's roof opens like a camera aperture, eight triangular panels sliding outward at once.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'atlanta-grady.webp'} alt="The Henry W. Grady monument, a bronze statue on a tall granite pedestal, in downtown Atlanta." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Henry W. Grady monument, Five Points.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'atlanta-stadium.webp'} alt="Mercedes-Benz Stadium with its angular roof, a construction crane beside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mercedes-Benz Stadium, from an overpass.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The station
    entrance at Five Points is decorated with giant painted X-shaped sculptures, which is as good a way as any to mark a
    spot. Then we rode back in time for the flight home to <strong>Los Angeles</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'marta.webp'} alt="A MARTA station entrance in downtown Atlanta with colorful sculptures." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Five Points, downtown Atlanta, for about twenty minutes.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'marta-map.webp'} alt="A MARTA rail system map inside a train car." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The way back to the airport.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>The hedgehog, and the magnets</h2>
  <p>
    Every trip needs a mascot, and ours chose himself in Vermont. The hedgehog rode along for two weeks: he posed with a
    slush in Albany and a matcha sundae in Pittsburgh, was left behind in Cleveland and rescued, and made it all the way
    home. His story doesn't end there, which is the sad and funny part: months later, on Halloween 2024, he came with us to
    <a href="/misc/san-diego">the San Diego Zoo</a>, and this time we lost him for good. So we did the only reasonable thing. We bought another one, the exact same
    toy, and pretended nothing had happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other souvenir was quieter. At stop after stop we picked up a fridge magnet: cheap, tacky, perfect. They're all
    on our refrigerator now, a cluttered map of the whole loop that we walk past every morning. There's Vermont, West
    Virginia from that half-hour in Wheeling, the mitten of Michigan, the shape of Wisconsin, a cassette tape, a little family of Simpsons, and many more, in no
    particular order, which is how memory works anyway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'fridge-magnets.webp'} alt="A refrigerator door covered in souvenir magnets from the trip." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The scrapbook.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Was it worth the hurry?</h2>
  <p>
    A city a day is not the way to know a place. It is the way to meet a lot of them. We got a handshake with two dozen
    towns and a real conversation with maybe three. But the handshakes add up to more than you'd think.
  </p>
  <p>
    Without ever planning it, we toured ten campuses: Yale, Brown, Harvard, MIT, Syracuse, Pitt, Carnegie Mellon, Ohio
    State, Case Western and the University of Chicago. Apparently a week at SIGGRAPH doesn't switch that part of the
    brain off. Seen back to back, they make a little history of American campus architecture. Brown's colonial brick
    and Syracuse's Victorian stone come first, then Yale's and Pitt's twentieth-century Gothic, and finally Gehry's
    melting brick and the glass of Carnegie Mellon's student center.
  </p>
  <p>
    We also saw a remarkable number of state capitols without meaning to: Colorado, Massachusetts, New York, Ohio,
    Michigan and Wisconsin. That says something about how American cities like to put their best building at the end
    of the main street. Line them up and you can see a whole national argument about what government should look like.
    There is Bulfinch's restrained gold dome in Boston, and Albany's French château. Columbus has its domeless Greek
    temple. Lansing and Denver are Myers's twins, and Madison is a white granite crown on its isthmus. Every one of them
    was built to say "this place matters", and every one of them, in its own way, does.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Great Lakes were the other surprise. We saw four of the five in four days: Ontario at Rochester, Erie at
    Presque Isle and Cleveland, Huron's waters passing Detroit in the river, and Michigan at Michigan City, Chicago and
    Milwaukee. They are so big that the eye refuses to treat them as lakes. They have beaches, horizons, lighthouses
    and storms. They also explain the whole region: why the cities are where they are, why the mills were built, and
    why, when the mills closed, the cities turned back to the water.
  </p>
  <p>
    Still, some states we saw for an afternoon, and West Virginia we saw for half an hour. People sometimes ask
    whether that even counts.
  </p>
  <p>
    I think it does. A few hours in a place is not the same as knowing it, but it is very far from never having been
    there at all. Before this trip, Wheeling was a name on a map. Now it is a particular street of red-brick
    storefronts on a bright afternoon, a cone in the road, and a magnet on the fridge. Syracuse is a hill of stone
    buildings in golden light. Cleveland is a dark sky over a white museum and the moment we realized the hedgehog was
    missing. None of these is a deep acquaintance, but each one is real, and each one is mine. The difference between
    "I passed through once" and "I've never been" turns out to be much bigger than the difference between "I passed
    through once" and "I know it well".
  </p>
  <p>
    I would happily go back to any of these places for a slower, deeper trip: a week in Chicago, a summer weekend in
    Vermont doing nothing at all, a proper day at Niagara. I hope I do. But nobody is promised a second chance, and
    the regret I would least like to have is not "I only had a few hours there". It is "I had the chance, and I
    didn't go". A hurried visit is enough to rule that one out. The slow trips can come later, if they come at all. The brief ones
    are the ones you can actually take, and they add up.
  </p>
  <p>
    And there's something to be said for the blur itself. A trip like this is mostly the in-between: rain on the
    windshield in Buffalo, the anvil clouds on I-79, a rest stop that sells hedgehogs. By the end we had set foot in
    fifteen states, thirteen of them by car, and every one of them is now a place rather than a word. Two weeks, one
    summer, the eastern half of the country out the windshield, and a toy hedgehog on the dashboard. No regrets.
  </p>
